      {
        type: "application",
        title: "Mary in Old Age: The Insanity Trial",
        content: "On May 19, 1875, ten years after her husband's murder and four years after Tad's death from pleurisy, Mary Todd Lincoln was put on trial for insanity in Chicago. The plaintiff was her own son. Robert Todd Lincoln, by then a successful attorney and future Secretary of War, had grown alarmed at his mother's behavior. She wandered Chicago hotels in mourning black, kept $57,000 in government bonds sewn into her petticoats, bought items in bulk she did not need (seventeen pairs of gloves in one trip), and spoke of voices, of plots to kill her, of having seen her father appear in a hotel mirror. Robert hired Pinkerton detectives to follow her. He gathered seventeen male witnesses. Mary was not told of the trial until the morning it began. The all-male jury deliberated for ten minutes. The verdict: insane. She was committed to Bellevue Place, a private asylum in Batavia, Illinois. There is an application here, but it is not a tidy one. Mary's behavior was strange, but she was also a woman who had buried three sons and held her husband as he was shot in the head. Modern historians, including Jean Baker, argue she was grief-stricken and eccentric, not insane. Mary herself fought back. She smuggled letters to the feminist lawyer Myra Bradwell. Within four months she was released. She lived her final years in Europe, then with her sister in Springfield, in the same house where her marriage began. The lesson is about how easily a difficult woman, especially a grieving one, was labeled mad — and about the price Mary paid for being Mary Todd Lincoln.",
        visual: {
          type: "diagram",
          svg: `<svg viewBox="0 0 1100 1100" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><rect width="1100" height="1100" fill="#1a1a2e"/><text x="550" y="60" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="32" font-weight="bold">The 1875 Insanity Trial</text><rect x="100" y="120" width="900" height="160" fill="#ef4444" opacity="0.2" stroke="#ef4444" stroke-width="2"/><text x="550" y="160" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ef4444" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">May 19, 1875: Cook County Courthouse</text><text x="550" y="195" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16">Plaintiff: Robert Todd Lincoln, 31, attorney, only surviving son</text><text x="550" y="225" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16">Defendant: Mary Todd Lincoln, 56, widow of the murdered president</text><text x="550" y="255" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16">She was told the morning of the trial. No lawyer of her choice.</text><rect x="100" y="320" width="430" height="340" fill="#6366f1" opacity="0.2" stroke="#6366f1" stroke-width="2"/><text x="315" y="360" text-anchor="middle" fill="#6366f1" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">Evidence of "insanity"</text><text x="120" y="400" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15">- Wandered hotels in mourning</text><text x="120" y="430" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15">- $57,000 bonds sewn in petticoats</text><text x="120" y="460" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15">- Bought 17 pairs of gloves at once</text><text x="120" y="490" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15">- Carried $1,000 cash at all times</text><text x="120" y="520" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15">- Heard voices in walls</text><text x="120" y="550" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15">- Saw her father in a mirror</text><text x="120" y="580" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15">- Believed assassins followed her</text><text x="120" y="610" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15">- Held seances for Willie</text><text x="120" y="640" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15">- 17 male witnesses for Robert</text><rect x="570" y="320" width="430" height="340" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.2" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="2"/><text x="785" y="360" text-anchor="middle" fill="#10b981" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">What we now know</text><text x="590" y="400" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15">- Buried 3 of 4 sons</text><text x="590" y="430" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15">- Husband murdered beside her</text><text x="590" y="460" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15">- Constant migraines since 20s</text><text x="590" y="490" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15">- Real assassination threats existed</text><text x="590" y="520" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15">- Cash because banks failed in 1873</text><text x="590" y="550" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15">- Spiritualism was widespread</text><text x="590" y="580" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15">- No defense lawyer she chose</text><text x="590" y="610" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15">- All-male jury</text><text x="590" y="640" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15">- Deliberated ten minutes</text><rect x="200" y="700" width="700" height="140" fill="#ffd700" opacity="0.2" stroke="#ffd700" stroke-width="3"/><text x="550" y="740" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">The fight back</text><text x="550" y="775" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16">Smuggled letters to Myra Bradwell, feminist attorney</text><text x="550" y="805" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16">Released after 4 months. Second trial declared her sane, 1876.</text><text x="550" y="900" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ec4899" font-size="20" font-style="italic">"Robert, you have driven your mother insane —</text><text x="550" y="930" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ec4899" font-size="20" font-style="italic">that will be your punishment."</text><text x="550" y="970" text-anchor="middle" fill="#888" font-size="16">— Mary's last letter to Robert. She died in 1882, never speaking to him.</text><text x="550" y="1040" text-anchor="middle" fill="#888" font-size="16">Grief-stricken? Yes. Eccentric? Certainly. Insane? Probably not.</text></svg>`,
          caption: "Robert had his mother committed. Mary fought back and won her freedom."
        }
      }
